& [RawDictation] is preserved without canonicalization
  + [text] is "so it was my impression that the system will be articulated like a DAG, and that having strong basins of identity coordinated in that DAG is a strong representation of bounded and articulate identity... as such, if we guard the Cantor system with an identity recognition layer that protects stable identity and only permits unstable objects as sandboxed elements under inspection, that this should fulfill the intended purposes of Honesty and Security?"

& [ImmediateContext] preserves the active design pressure
  + GroundedIdentityIntegrity is the current stronger common faculty-level primitive candidate for Honesty and Security
  + Cantor already recognizes digest-bound compiled packages and queries an immutable admitted semantic fabric
  + current IdentityBoundary machine forms are static and do not yet express identity transition or candidate promotion
  - never: treat this context as canonical interpretation of the raw wording

& [UnresolvedTerms] remain deliberately open
  + [DAG] may apply to derivation, revision, dependency, proof, admission, or execution order rather than every semantic relation
  + [identity_basin] may mean a stable identity key coordinated with immutable revisions, grounding invariants, typed relations, and proof
  + [stable_identity] may mean continuity of a durable key rather than immutability of all state
  + [unstable_object] may mean ungrounded candidate, proposed transition, unresolved observation, mutable working state, or adversarial input
  + [sandbox] may require logical non-admission, capability denial, physical process isolation, or all three under different threat models
  + [fulfill] may mean provide the common enforcement substrate or completely perform both faculty judgments

